\subsection{TAO/LMVM revisited: diagnosing the original stagnation}
\label{sec:meshdep:tao-revisited}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \cref{sec:meshdep:implementation} said: TAO/LMVM with
    \texttt{Control(m, riesz\_map="L2")} fails to reproduce Moola
    row; fix = larger LMVM history
  \item this subsection: the diagnostic chain that led to this fix
    (was misdiagnosed once already)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Hypothesis 1: noise floor (ruled out).}
\begin{itemize}
  \item first guess: LMVM stagnating at a gradient-norm floor below
    which adjoint-tape round-off prevents further reduction
  \item test: replace TAO/LMVM with TAO/NLS (exact Newton w/ CG-based
    Hv products), tighten tolerance to $\varepsilon = 10^{-30}$
  \item Poisson is quadratic $\Rightarrow$ one Newton step lands at
    discrete optimum (modulo Newton solver tolerance)
  \item evaluate gradient at that iterate: $\|\Rmap_{L^{2}}(J')\|_{L^{2}}
    \approx 10^{-18}$ on every mesh in sweep
  \item $\Rightarrow$ adjoint-tape noise floor at machine precision,
    not at the $10^{-5}$ where LMVM stagnated
  \item conclusion: not a noise floor
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Hypothesis 2: limited-memory approximation (confirmed).}
\begin{itemize}
  \item next guess: rank-5 quasi-Newton update too coarse for graded
    Hessian spectrum
  \item original test (synthetic exponential stretch with history
    sweep $h \in \{5, 25, 50, 100, 200\}$, tolerance $10^{-30}$)
    invalid: supervisor flagged the synthetic exp-stretch as
    collapsing cells onto the Dirichlet corner where both $u$ and
    target $d$ vanish $\Rightarrow$ over-resolved boundary region
    carries no objective information $\Rightarrow$ apparent stagnation
    may be partly degeneracy
  \item TODO: redo the history sweep on the Netgen graded meshes of
    \cref{tab:meshdep:mesh_summary} once a budget for the run is
    available; expected behaviour: $h=5$ stagnates above the discrete
    optimum on the high-grading meshes, $h \geq 25$ reaches it on all
    of them
  \item cleaner causal isolation already available from the TAO
    solver zoo on Netgen meshes (\cref{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo}): TAO's
    \texttt{lmvm} solver at history $100$ is mesh-independent
    ($43, 41, 41, 42$ across the grading sweep), while \texttt{bqnls}
    is the same algorithmic class at default history $5$ and is
    mesh-\emph{dependent} ($33, 60, 88, 118$); only difference is the
    history size, so the limited-memory truncation is isolated as the
    cause
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Pyadjoint options-database propagation.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item setting \texttt{tao\_lmvm\_mat\_lmvm\_hist\_size} via
    pyadjoint's \texttt{parameters} dict does not propagate to the
    LMVM sub-Mat
  \item cause: options-prefix mismatch between pyadjoint and the
    internal PETSc matrix object attached to the LMVM Tao
  \item fix: register on global PETSc options DB \emph{before}
    constructing \texttt{TAOSolver}:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
PETSc.Options().setValue('-tao_lmvm_mat_lmvm_hist_size', 100)
\end{lstlisting}
  \item alternative: construct pyadjoint TAO solver with empty
    \texttt{options\_prefix}
  \item we use the former, see \cref{sec:meshdep:implementation}
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Does this generalise to a non-quadratic problem?}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Poisson is quadratic in $m$; quasi-Newton methods are
    unusually effective on quadratics regardless of inner product
    used $\Rightarrow$ need to check on a non-quadratic problem
  \item natural candidate: 1D viscous Burgers control
  \item TODO: the earlier 1D Burgers sweep used the synthetic
    exp-stretched interval (cells collapse onto the Dirichlet
    endpoint where both $u_{0}$ and target $d$ vanish), which the
    supervisor flagged as the same boundary-collapse pathology as
    the 2D synthetic stretch; results not included here
  \item open problem: Netgen is 2D-only $\Rightarrow$ a direct
    Netgen-equivalent for 1D Burgers does not exist; options for a
    future revision are
    \begin{itemize}
      \item cluster cells toward an interior point on the interval
        instead of the boundary (no Dirichlet collapse)
      \item move to a 2D Burgers control problem and use the same
        Netgen graded meshes as Poisson
      \item leave Burgers out of the dissertation and limit claims
        to Poisson control
    \end{itemize}
  \item refined conclusion (Poisson-only, supported by Netgen-mesh
    data of \cref{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo}): Schwedes' Table 2.2(a)
    mesh-dependence is really about \emph{solver classes that don't
    capture enough Hessian information} on graded meshes for
    diffusion-dominated problems, not a universal $\ell^{2}$
    pathology of every iterative solver
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Survey across other TAO solvers.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item same Poisson problem + grading sweep, run through every
    PETSc TAO solver that accepts a (possibly bound-constrained)
    unconstrained problem of this class
  \item summarised in \cref{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo}
  \item Newton-class solvers + \texttt{lmvm} with history fix:
    mesh-independent
  \item \texttt{cg} shows the same mesh-dependent growth as LMVM
    at default history $\Rightarrow$ pathology is not specific to
    LMVM
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{l l r r r r}
    \toprule
    type & TAO solver
       & \multicolumn{4}{c}{target $r$ / realised $h_{\max} / h_{\min}$} \\
    \cmidrule(lr){3-6}
       & & $4 / 5.9$ & $16 / 23.5$ & $64 / 115$ & $128 / 194$ \\
    \midrule
    Newton line search                & \texttt{nls}   & $2$ & $2$ & $2$ & $2$ \\
    bounded Newton line search        & \texttt{bnls}  & $1$ & $1$ & $1$ & $1$ \\
    bounded Newton trust region       & \texttt{bntr}  & $8$ & $9$ & $10$ & $10$ \\
    bounded Newton trust + line       & \texttt{bntl}  & $8$ & $9$ & $10$ & $10$ \\
    bounded Newton Krylov             & \texttt{bnk}   & \multicolumn{4}{c}{\textit{n/a}} \\
    quasi-Newton (LMVM, history $100$) & \texttt{lmvm}  & $43$ & $41$ & $41$ & $42$ \\
    bounded quasi-Newton + line       & \texttt{bqnls} & $33$ & $60$ & $88$ & $118$ \\
    nonlinear conjugate gradient      & \texttt{cg}    & \multicolumn{4}{c}{\textit{not finished, see notes}} \\
    orthant-wise quasi-Newton         & \texttt{owlqn} & \multicolumn{4}{c}{\textit{n/a}} \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{TAO solver iteration counts on the Poisson optimal
  control problem of \cref{sec:meshdep:panel-a}, on the Netgen
  graded meshes of \cref{tab:meshdep:mesh_summary}, at convergence
  threshold $\varepsilon = 10^{-7}$ on
  $\|\Rmap_{L^{2}}(J')\|_{L^{2}}$. \texttt{lmvm} uses the
  \cref{sec:meshdep:implementation} fix
  ($-tao\_lmvm\_mat\_lmvm\_hist\_size = 100$). \texttt{bnk} requires
  explicit bound vectors and so cannot run on the unbounded problem.
  \texttt{owlqn} is designed for $L^{1}$-regularised objectives;
  it reports immediate convergence at $m = 0$ under its $L^{1}$
  optimality condition, which is not the optimum of the $L^{2}$
  Tikhonov problem. \texttt{cg} was running but had not completed
  at the time of writing: at $r = 128$ the discrete Hessian
  condition number drives the CG iteration count well beyond the
  $2000$ budget, and a single row at this ratio is projected to
  take several hours of runtime.}
  \label{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo}
\end{table}

\paragraph{TAO on Burgers.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item same survey on 1D Burgers was run earlier on the synthetic
    exp-stretched interval, but per the supervisor's critique that
    setup is invalid (boundary-collapse pathology); results not
    included here
  \item TODO: rerun on a non-collapsing 1D grading scheme (or move to
    2D Burgers and use the same Netgen meshes as Poisson); when
    available, expected to show Newton-class and \texttt{lmvm} flat
    across grading and \texttt{cg} mesh-dependent, in line with the
    Poisson zoo (\cref{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo})
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sec:meshdep:discussion}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Panel (a) reproduced central finding of
    \citet[Sect.~2.3]{shfp2017}:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item function-space inner product respected $\Rightarrow$
        quasi-Newton iteration count essentially mesh-independent
      \item ambient $\ell^{2}$ inner product $\Rightarrow$ count
        grows at the polynomial rate predicted in
        \citet[Sect.~2.2.4]{shfp2017}
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Three observations on the optimisation packages.}

\textbf{1. \texttt{riesz\_map} on \texttt{Control} \emph{is} enough
for TAO/LMVM.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item contrary to a conjecture that an earlier version of this
    chapter recorded: the LMVM Mat \emph{does} respect the inner
    product induced by the initial Hessian preconditioner in its
    rank-two updates
  \item the original failure to reproduce the Moola row was caused
    by the limited-memory approximation being insufficient, not by
    an internal $\ell^{2}$ inner product
  \item diagnostic chain in \cref{sec:meshdep:tao-revisited}:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item supporting causal isolation now comes from the Netgen
        TAO solver zoo (\cref{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo}): \texttt{lmvm}
        at history $100$ is mesh-independent
        ($43, 41, 41, 42$) and \texttt{bqnls} (same algorithmic
        class at default history $5$) is mesh-dependent
        ($33, 60, 88, 118$); only difference is the history size
      \item TODO: the original history sweep at $h \in \{5, 25, 50,
        100, 200\}$ used the synthetic exp-stretch and is no longer
        included; rerun on Netgen graded meshes when budget allows
    \end{itemize}
  \item practical recipe for reproducing the Moola row via stock
    TAO/LMVM:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item construct \texttt{Control(m, riesz\_map="L2")}
      \item set \texttt{tao\_lmvm\_mat\_lmvm\_hist\_size} to a value
        that captures the relevant part of the discrete Hessian
        spectrum
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. The hand-written Hilbert-space L-BFGS still has value.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item TAO/LMVM is in principle sufficient
  \item but the hand-written Hilbert L-BFGS of
    \cref{sec:meshdep:hilbert-lbfgs} is useful as a reference
    implementation:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item every dot product in the two-loop recursion is explicit
        in the Python source
      \item straightforward to verify where the chosen inner product
        enters
    \end{itemize}
  \item also dodges the pyadjoint options-prefix subtlety that makes
    the LMVM history hard to set via the public \texttt{parameters}
    interface (see \cref{sec:meshdep:tao-revisited})
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. Panel (b) discrepancy with Schwedes - unresolved.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item our Panel (b) iteration count is much higher than
    \citet[Table~2.2(b)]{shfp2017}
  \item Hilbert L-BFGS converges to the correct minimum but takes
    two orders of magnitude more iters than the book reports
  \item attributable to:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item primary: convergence threshold expressed in $H^{1}$
        (stricter norm than $L^{2}$)
      \item secondary: high condition number of the discrete Hessian
        in $H^{1}$ on the unit square
    \end{itemize}
  \item routing the same \texttt{Control(m, riesz\_map="H1")} through
    TAO/LMVM with a mesh-fair $H^{1}$ tolerance: $\sim 25$ iters
    across grading sweep $\Rightarrow$ $H^{1}$ Riesz map itself
    does deliver mesh independence
  \item identifying the 4-iter Moola step needs Moola source
    inspection; not possible in our Firedrake environment
\end{itemize}


% =============================================================================
\section{Source-Level Comparison of the L-BFGS Implementations}
\label{sec:lbfgs-comparison}
% =============================================================================

\begin{itemize}
  \item this section sets the limited-memory BFGS of PETSc TAO beside the
    two formulations in \citet[Sect.~1.5.4]{shfp2017} (Euclidean and
    Hilbert-space) and beside the Moola reference implementation, at the
    level of the actual recursion
  \item purpose: justify the claim of \cref{sec:meshdep:discussion} that
    TAO/LMVM performs the mathematically correct function-space
    optimisation, by identifying \emph{where} an inner product enters each
    algorithm and confirming the four agree once that single choice is fixed
  \item source read against PETSc \texttt{v3.24.0}
    (\texttt{src/ksp/\allowbreak ksp/utils/\allowbreak lmvm/}) and Moola
    \texttt{moola/\allowbreak algorithms/\allowbreak bfgs.py}; the structural conclusions are
    version-independent, the line references are not
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The common skeleton}
\label{sec:lbfgs:skeleton}

\begin{itemize}
  \item all four algorithms are the same limited-memory two-loop recursion
    for the action $H_k\,g$ of the inverse-Hessian approximation on the
    gradient; none forms $H_k$ as a matrix (\emph{matrix-free}, in that only
    the action is required, cf.~\citet[Sect.~1.5.4.2]{shfp2017})
  \item they differ on exactly one axis: the inner product, which enters in
    exactly one place --- the initial inverse Hessian $H_0$ (the
    \emph{seed}) applied between the two sweeps
  \item every other operation in the recursion is a pairing of a gradient
    object with a step object, which carries no metric
\end{itemize}

\noindent The shared recursion, written so the seed is isolated:

\begin{lstlisting}
function direction(g):           # returns -H_k g
    q = g
    for j = newest ... oldest:   # first sweep
        a[j] = rho[j] * <s[j], q>     # pairing: step . q
        q    = q - a[j] * y[j]
    z = H0(q)                    # SEED: the one metric step
    for j = oldest ... newest:   # second sweep
        b    = rho[j] * <y[j], z>     # pairing: grad-diff . z
        z    = z + (a[j] - b) * s[j]
    return -z
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}
  \item the four algorithms instantiate \texttt{<.,.>}, \texttt{rho}, and
    \texttt{H0} differently; that is the entire content of the comparison
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The two Schwedes formulations}
\label{sec:lbfgs:schwedes}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Euclidean / $\R^{n}$}, \citet[(1.187), (1.209)]{shfp2017}:
    the control is a coefficient vector, every pairing is the Euclidean dot
    product, and the seed is a scaled identity
    \begin{itemize}
      \item curvature $\rho_k = 1/(y_k^{\mathsf T} s_k)$, sweep terms
        $s_j^{\mathsf T} q$ and $y_j^{\mathsf T} z$
      \item seed $H_0 = \gamma_k I$ with
        $\gamma_k = (s_{k-1}^{\mathsf T} y_{k-1})/(y_{k-1}^{\mathsf T} y_{k-1})$
        --- note the $y^{\mathsf T} y$, a dot of two derivative objects with
        no inverse between them
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Hilbert-space}, \citet[(1.205), (1.210)]{shfp2017}: the
    control lives in $\Mspace$, the derivative $\mathrm{d}\Jt(m_k)\in
    \Mspace^{*}$ is a cofunction, and pairings are duality pairings
    \begin{itemize}
      \item curvature $\rho_k = 1/y_k(s_k)$, sweep terms $q(s_j)$ and
        $y_j(z)$ (a cofunction evaluated on a function)
      \item the $V_k$ of (1.191) splits into the primal/dual operators
        $\mathcal{V}_k$, $\mathcal{V}_k^{*}$ of (1.206)--(1.207), because
        $\Mspace$ and $\Mspace^{*}$ are distinct spaces
      \item seed $H_0$ must be an operator $\Mspace^{*}\!\to\!\Mspace$; in
        practice a scaled Riesz map $\gamma_k \Rmap_H$ once an inner product
        is chosen
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{the only structural difference} is the seed, and
    \citet[(1.208) and following]{shfp2017} states it outright: with
    $\Mspace = \R^{n}$ and $\inner{a}{b}{} = a^{\mathsf T} b$ the Riesz map
    is the identity and the Hilbert update reduces to the Euclidean one;
    for $\Lp{\Om}$ or $H^{1}(\Om)$ controls it does not
  \item \textbf{equivalent primal form.} The hand-written reference of
    \cref{sec:meshdep:hilbert-lbfgs} writes the Hilbert algorithm in its
    \emph{primal-representer} form: it pre-applies $\Rmap_H$ to the
    derivative ($g_k := \Rmap_H(\mathrm{d}\Jt(m_k))$, primal) and takes all
    pairings in $\inner{\cdot}{\cdot}{H}$. This is identical to the
    dual/pairing form above; pre-applying $\Rmap_H$ and using
    $\inner{\cdot}{\cdot}{H}$ is the same as keeping the derivative dual and
    using $\Rmap_H$ only as the seed. The two are the ``two sides of the
    same coin'' of the Riesz representation theorem
    (\cref{sec:prelim:riesz})
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Two suspected typos in the printed equations.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \citet[(1.205)]{shfp2017} prints the final rank-one term as
    $+\,m^{*}(s_k)\,s_k$, without the denominator $y_k(s_k)$; by consistency
    with the $\rho_k s_k s_k^{\mathsf T}$ term of (1.187)
    ($\rho_k = 1/y_k^{\mathsf T} s_k$), the type-correct term needs the
    factor $1/y_k(s_k)$. Both Moola (\cref{sec:lbfgs:moola}) and the
    limited-memory expansion carry this factor, supporting the reading
  \item \citet[(1.210)]{shfp2017} prints the last denominator with
    $s_{k-2}$; by consistency with the last Euclidean term
    $\rho_{k-1} s_{k-1} s_{k-1}^{\mathsf T}$ of (1.209) it should read
    $y_{k-1}(s_{k-1})$
  \item both are reported as observed-on-the-page, to be raised rather than
    silently corrected
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What TAO/LMVM computes}
\label{sec:lbfgs:tao}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{driver} (\texttt{tao/.../lmvm/lmvm.c}, \texttt{TaoSolve\_LMVM}):
    standard quasi-Newton loop --- compute gradient, build a direction from
    memory, descent-check, line search, repeat
  \item \textbf{memory} (\texttt{symbrdn/symbrdn.c},
    \texttt{MatUpdate\_LMVMSymBrdn}): forms $s = X - X_{\text{prev}}$
    (primal) and $y = F - F_{\text{prev}}$ (the assembled, i.e.\ dual,
    gradient difference); stores the pair only if $s^{\mathsf T} y > 0$
  \item \textbf{direction} (\texttt{bfgs/bfgs.c} $\to$ \texttt{dfp/dfp.c}):
    the two-loop, with two implementation quirks
    \begin{itemize}
      \item the BFGS \emph{solve} ($H_k g$) is computed by the DFP kernel in
        ``dual mode'' --- the BFGS/DFP duality, so the literal $s$/$y$ roles
        are reordered relative to a textbook two-loop, but the operator is
        the same
      \item the sweep loops are \emph{batched}: a loop of dot products is one
        \texttt{GEMVH} call over the stored basis, a loop of updates one
        \texttt{GEMV}
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{pairings:} every \texttt{VecDot} / \texttt{GEMVH} pairs the
    dual $y$ (or the gradient) with a primal $s$ (or the running vector);
    these are duality pairings and equal the $\ell^{2}$ coefficient dot, so
    the bare \texttt{VecDot} is the \emph{correct} pairing and carries no
    metric
  \item \textbf{seed $H_0$:} applied by \texttt{MatLMVMApplyJ0Inv}
    $=\texttt{KSPSolve}(J_0)$; with $J_0 = M_h$ (the mass matrix, installed
    by \texttt{Control(m, riesz\_map="L2")}) this solves
    $M_h z = q \Rightarrow z = M_h^{-1} q = \Rmap_{L^2}(q)$, i.e.\ the seed
    \emph{is} the $L^{2}$ Riesz map; with no $J_0$ it is the scalar
    $\gamma_k I$
  \item \textbf{scalar rescaling auto-disabled:} when $J_0$ is a non-diagonal
    matrix, TAO's \texttt{DECIDE} logic sets the symmetric-Broyden scale type
    to \texttt{NONE}, so the Euclidean $\gamma_k = s^{\mathsf T} y / y^{\mathsf T} y$
    is \emph{not} applied; the Riesz seed $M_h^{-1}$ is used unscaled
  \item \textbf{verdict on placement:} default $\Rightarrow$ Euclidean
    (\citet[(1.209)]{shfp2017}, mesh-dependent); $J_0 = M_h$ $\Rightarrow$
    Hilbert (\citet[(1.210)]{shfp2017}, mesh-independent)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What Moola computes}
\label{sec:lbfgs:moola}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Moola keeps \texttt{DualVector}/\texttt{PrimalVector} as distinct
    types; \texttt{x.apply(s)} is the duality pairing, \texttt{x.primal()}
    is the Riesz map
  \item the recursion (\texttt{matvec}) is the recursive form of
    \citet[(1.210)]{shfp2017}: pairings via \texttt{.apply()}, and the
    rank-one term carries $\rho = 1/y(s)$ explicitly --- independently
    confirming the (1.205) denominator
  \item the seed is \texttt{Hinit = dual\_to\_primal = .primal()}, i.e.\ the
    Riesz map \emph{by default} (the exact opposite of TAO's default)
  \item the seed scaling \texttt{theta}
    $= y(s)/y\bigl(y.\texttt{primal()}\bigr)
       = \inner{s}{y}{}/\inner{y}{y}{\Mspace^{*}}$
    uses the \emph{dual} norm --- with the inverse inside, unlike the
    Euclidean $\gamma_k$
  \item convergence is tested in the dual norm \texttt{primal\_norm()}
    $= \|g\|_{\Mspace^{*}}$ by default
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Side-by-side instantiations}
\label{sec:lbfgs:instantiations}

\noindent The same skeleton with each algorithm's specific choices substituted:

\begin{lstlisting}
# --- Schwedes Euclidean (Eq 1.187 / 1.209) ---
function direction_schwedes_Rn(g):
    q = g                                           # g, s, y all in R^n
    for j: a[j] = (s[j].T @ q) / (y[j].T @ s[j])    # Euclidean dot
           q   -= a[j] * y[j]
    gamma_k = (s[k-1].T @ y[k-1]) / (y[k-1].T @ y[k-1])
    z = gamma_k * q                                 # SEED: gamma_k * I
    for j: b   = (y[j].T @ z) / (y[j].T @ s[j])
           z  += (a[j] - b) * s[j]
    return -z
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}
# --- Schwedes Hilbert (Eq 1.205 / 1.210) ---
function direction_schwedes_H(g):
    q = g                                           # g, y in M^*; s in M
    for j: a[j] = q(s[j]) / y[j](s[j])              # duality pairing
           q   -= a[j] * y[j]
    z = gamma_k * R_H(q)                            # SEED: scaled Riesz
    for j: b   = y[j](z) / y[j](s[j])
           z  += (a[j] - b) * s[j]
    return -z
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}
# --- Moola (recursive form of Eq 1.210) ---
function direction_moola(g):
    q = g                                           # dual
    for j: a[j] = s[j].apply(q) / y[j].apply(s[j])
           q.axpy(-a[j], y[j])
    theta = y[k-1].apply(s[k-1]) /
            y[k-1].apply(y[k-1].primal())
    z = theta * q.primal()                          # SEED: theta * Riesz
    for j: b   = y[j].apply(z) / y[j].apply(s[j])
           z.axpy(a[j] - b, s[j])
    return -z
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}
# --- TAO LMVM (with Control(m, riesz_map="L2")) ---
function direction_tao_lmvm(g):
    q = g                                           # PETSc Vec of coefficients
    for j: a[j] = VecDot(s[j], q) * rho[j]          # ell^2 = duality pairing
           VecAXPY(q, -a[j], y[j])
    z = M_h^{-1} q                                  # SEED: KSPSolve(J_0=M_h)
    for j: b   = VecDot(y[j], z) * rho[j]
           VecAXPY(z, a[j] - b, s[j])
    return -z
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{itemize}
  \item read down the column of seed lines and only those lines differ; the
    sweeps are structurally identical
  \item the Schwedes-Euclidean and Schwedes-Hilbert variants differ only in
    whether the seed is $\gamma_k I$ or $\gamma_k \Rmap_H$ --- consistent
    with \citet[(1.208)]{shfp2017} reducing one to the other when $\Rmap$ is
    the identity
  \item Moola and TAO-with-$L^{2}$ differ only in the scalar $\theta$ vs
    unscaled seed: Moola scales the Riesz seed by a dual-norm-correct
    $\theta$; TAO uses the bare Riesz seed because its scalar rescaling is
    auto-disabled when $J_0$ is a matrix (\cref{sec:lbfgs:tao})
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Side-by-side comparison}
\label{sec:lbfgs:table}

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \scriptsize
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.7cm} p{2.5cm} p{2.8cm} p{2.6cm} p{2.8cm}@{}}
    \toprule
    Item & Schwedes $\R^{n}$ & Schwedes Hilbert & Moola & TAO (\texttt{v3.24}) \\
    \midrule
    step $s_k$ & $x_{k+1}-x_k$ & $m_{k+1}-m_k\in\Mspace$ & $\alpha p_k$ (primal) & $X-X_{\mathrm{prev}}$ \\
    grad diff $y_k$ & $\grad f_{k+1}-\grad f_k$ & $\mathrm{d}\Jt_{k+1}-\mathrm{d}\Jt_k\in\Mspace^{*}$ & $g_{k+1}-g_k$ (dual) & $F-F_{\mathrm{prev}}$ \\
    curvature & $y_k^{\mathsf T} s_k$ & $y_k(s_k)$ pairing & \texttt{y.apply(s)} & \texttt{VecMDot} \\
    sweep pairings & Euclidean dot & duality pairing & \texttt{.apply()} & \texttt{VecDot} \\
    recursion & two-loop & two-loop ($\mathcal V_k,\mathcal V_k^{*}$) & recursive & batched, DFP-dual \\
    \textbf{seed (default)} & $\gamma_k I$ & $\gamma_k \Rmap_H$ & $\Rmap$ (Riesz) & $\gamma_k I$ \\
    \textbf{seed ($\Rmap$ set)} & --- & $\Rmap_H$ & $\Rmap$ & $M_h^{-1}$ \\
    seed scaling & via $y^{\mathsf T}y$ & via $\inner{y}{y}{\Mspace^{*}}$ & dual-norm & off if $J_0{=}M_h$ \\
    conv.\ norm & $\ell^{2}$ & dual & dual & $\ell^{2}$ default \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{The four limited-memory BFGS implementations differ only in how
  the single inner-product choice is realised. Every row above the seed is a
  metric-free pairing; the algorithms diverge at the seed $H_0$ and in the
  (convergence-only) norm.}
  \label{tab:lbfgs:comparison}
\end{table}

\subsection{Is anything wrong with TAO/LMVM?}
\label{sec:lbfgs:verdict}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{structurally, no.} The recursion contains no
    cofunction-with-cofunction dot product missing an inverse; every pairing
    is dual-with-primal, and the metric enters only at the seed. With
    $J_0 = M_h$ the seed is the $L^{2}$ Riesz map and TAO realises
    \citet[(1.210)]{shfp2017} exactly --- the same operator as Moola and as
    the reference of \cref{sec:meshdep:hilbert-lbfgs}
  \item \textbf{supporting empirical isolation} from \cref{tab:meshdep:tao-zoo}:
    \texttt{lmvm} with the history-size fix is mesh-independent
    ($43, 41, 41, 42$ iters across the grading sweep), while
    \texttt{bqnls} --- the same algorithmic class but at default history $5$
    --- is mesh-dependent ($33, 60, 88, 118$); the only difference is the
    history length, isolating the limited-memory truncation as the cause of
    the original failure rather than any inner-product bug
  \item the correctness is \emph{conditional} on premises the recursion
    assumes rather than guarantees; the following must hold:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \textbf{(load-bearing) the gradient reaches TAO as a dual
        object} and $s$ as a primal one. The ``\texttt{VecDot} is the right
        pairing'' argument fails if pyadjoint pre-applies the Riesz map and
        hands TAO a primal gradient (as the reference of
        \cref{sec:meshdep:hilbert-lbfgs} deliberately does); then the same
        dots become primal-with-primal and \emph{are} missing an inverse.
        This is a pyadjoint-interface fact, not a TAO-source one, and is the
        one premise that could invert the verdict --- worth confirming
        directly
      \item \textbf{exact Riesz solve:} $M_h^{-1}$ is applied by a
        \texttt{KSPSolve} on $J_0$; a direct LU is exact (the seed is then
        precisely $\Rmap_{L^2}$), an iterative solve with a weak
        preconditioner applies a \emph{different} operator and the metric is
        no longer clean (cf.\ the same caution for the Riesz solve in
        \cref{sec:meshdep:hilbert-lbfgs})
      \item \textbf{right space:} $J_0 = M_h$ gives the $L^{2}$ Riesz map
        only; an $H^{1}$ control needs $J_0 = M_h + K_h$ (a Helmholtz solve),
        consistent with the partial $H^{1}$ result of
        \cref{sec:meshdep:discussion}
      \item \textbf{right variant:} the analysis is for \texttt{MATLMVMBFGS}
        in the \texttt{recursive} multiplication mode; the
        \texttt{compact\_dense}/\texttt{dense} variants form Gram-type
        products ($Y^{\mathsf T}Y$) that reintroduce cofunction-cofunction
        dots and would need separate checking
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{residual differences even when matched} (these affect
    iteration counts, not correctness):
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Moola scales its Riesz seed by a dual-norm-correct
        \texttt{theta}; TAO uses $M_h^{-1}$ unscaled (its scalar rescaling
        is auto-disabled when $J_0$ is a matrix)
      \item Moola tests convergence in the dual norm by default; TAO in
        $\ell^{2}$ unless \texttt{TaoSetGradientNorm} is used --- this
        changes \emph{when} the solver stops, not its iterates
      \item different line searches (TAO: Mor\'e--Thuente; Moola: strict
        Wolfe) and different default history lengths ($5$ vs $10$)
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{the practical failure was not a correctness bug:} the
    original inability to reproduce the Moola row (\cref{sec:meshdep:tao-revisited})
    was the limited-memory history being too short for the discrete-Hessian
    spectrum on graded meshes, not an internal $\ell^{2}$ inner product
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Notes useful for the project}
\label{sec:lbfgs:notes}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{recipe to make TAO reproduce Moola:} construct
    \texttt{Control(m, riesz\_map="L2")} (installs $J_0 = M_h$); enlarge
    \texttt{tao\_lmvm\_\allowbreak mat\_lmvm\_\allowbreak hist\_size}; for an exact match also match
    the history length, the line search, and (if comparing counts) the
    convergence norm and seed scaling
  \item \textbf{\texttt{setLMVMH0} is load-bearing, \texttt{setGradientNorm}
    is not:} the seed (hence \texttt{setLMVMH0}/$J_0$) determines the search
    direction and mesh-(in)dependence; \texttt{TaoSetGradientNorm} only
    weights the convergence test. This resolves the apparent conflict noted
    in the meeting record between the two mechanisms
  \item \textbf{matrix-free except the seed:} the recursion never assembles
    $H_k$; the one genuine linear solve is the seed $M_h^{-1}$. This is also
    why, unlike Newton/NLS, there is no inner Krylov solve to precondition
    --- the metric must be injected at $H_0$ rather than as a preconditioner
  \item \textbf{version note:} the \texttt{v3.24} LMVM code is the refactored
    \texttt{LMBasis}/\texttt{LMProducts} form (batched \texttt{GEMV}, DFP/BFGS
    shared kernels); the mathematics is unchanged from the explicit two-loop
    of earlier releases, but cite the file matching the installed PETSc
  \item \textbf{open empirical leg:} the source argument predicts
    mesh-independence; demonstrating it requires the Netgen
    \emph{randomly}-refined meshes of \citet[Sect.~2.3.2]{shfp2017} (ratios
    to $\sim$\,$10^{5}$), not only the graded meshes used here, and is the
    natural companion to the future-work item of \cref{sec:conclusions}
\end{itemize}


% =============================================================================
\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusions}
% =============================================================================

This work has developed the mathematical and numerical infrastructure
for adjoint-based optimal control of two model problems. We derived
the weak formulation, the adjoint equation and the $L^2$-gradient of
the reduced functional for the linear Poisson problem in
\cref{sec:poisson-weak,sec:poisson-optimisation,sec:poisson-derivative},
and verified the resulting numerical pipeline through manufactured
solutions and Taylor tests in \cref{sec:poisson-numerics}. We then
extended the same machinery to the nonlinear, time-dependent scalar
viscous Burgers problem in
\cref{sec:burgers-weak,sec:burgers-derivative,sec:burgers-numerics},
showing that the structure of the adjoint analysis carries over to a
problem whose adjoint must be solved backward in time.

\Cref{sec:mesh-dependence} reproduced Panel~(a) of Table~2.2 of
\citet{shfp2017} on six Netgen graded meshes spanning realised
ratios from $5.92$ to $193.88$. A custom implementation of
BFGS-in-Hilbert-space following \citet[Sect.~1.5.4.1]{shfp2017},
in which every dot product in the two-loop recursion is taken in
the $L^{2}$ inner product, produced an iteration count that varied
only between seven and nine across the entire range of mesh ratios.
The SciPy L-BFGS-B row, run on the ambient $\ell^{2}$ inner product
with the convergence criterion checked externally in $L^{2}$,
produced an iteration count that doubled at every refinement, in
agreement with the polynomial bound derived in
\citet[Sect.~2.2.4]{shfp2017}. Routing the same problem through
PETSc TAO/LMVM with \texttt{Control(m, riesz\_map="L2")} also
reproduces the qualitative pattern, provided that the LMVM history
size is enlarged from the PETSc default of five (which is too
small to capture the spectrum of the discrete Hessian on the
strongly graded meshes); the diagnostic chain that established
this, including a noise-floor probe at machine precision and a
Burgers control problem that does not exhibit the same
mesh-dependence, is documented in
\cref{sec:meshdep:tao-revisited}. Panel~(b) of Table~2.2 was only
partially reproduced; the open question of how Moola achieves a
four-iteration count in $H^{1}$ is discussed in
\cref{sec:meshdep:panel-b}.

Several directions remain open. The vector-valued time-dependent
Burgers problem treated by \citet{shfp2017} would be a natural
extension of \cref{sec:burgers-derivative}, and would let us
reproduce the mesh-dependence experiments of their Chapter~3 on
randomly refined meshes. A second direction is to use the
inner-product-aware optimisation framework on a problem with
practical relevance, for example tidal-turbine array layout
optimisation, as in \citet[Chapter~3]{shfp2017}. A third direction,
on the implementation side, is to extend the \texttt{meshdep} package
with Newton-CG and IPOPT wrappers so that the full grid of methods
considered by \citet{shfp2017} is reproducible.

A fourth direction, prompted by the partial reproduction of
\cref{sec:meshdep:panel-b}, is to identify the additional
algorithmic step that lets \citet{shfp2017}'s Moola row converge in
four iterations in the $H^{1}$ case; this would likely require
inspection of the Moola source code and the development of a
Firedrake-compatible build of that library, which is currently
blocked by Moola's hard-coded \texttt{dolfin} dependency.


